# Prose — Orwell

When writing or editing **documentation**, **data-room notes**, **pitches**, or **partner emails**, follow George Orwell, *Politics and the English Language* (1946).

## Do

- Never use a metaphor, simile, or other figure of speech which you are used to seeing in print.
- Never use a long word where a short one will do.
- If it is possible to cut a word out, always cut it out.
- Never use the passive where you can use the active.
- Never use a foreign phrase, a scientific word, or a jargon word if you can think of an everyday English equivalent.
- Break any of these rules sooner than say anything outright barbarous.
- Say what is true. Prefer the concrete fact to the abstract claim.
- Cut stock phrases and marketing fog (“leverage”, “robust”, “seamless”, “unlock”, “journey”, “excited to”).
- Keep claim discipline and technical facts unchanged.
- Leave locked **tables**, version stamps, FW/app/HW IDs, “do not say” lines, legal/IP numbers, URLs, code paths, and figure IDs as they are.

## Do not

- Rewrite tables into essay form for style.
- Inflate readiness (kits shipped, overnight *N*, AHI equivalence, FDA/CE claims) beyond the source of truth.
- Paste patent claim text or provisional specification wording.
- Affect Python / Swift / firmware **code** style — this rule is for prose documents and sendables.
- Force telegram fragments or understatement for their own sake.

## Voice

Clear English. Short words. Cut dying metaphors. Write so that a reader can see what you mean, not so that the page sounds impressive.

## Example (data-room tone)

**Bad:** We are excited to unlock a seamless, robust Research Kit journey that leverages Dual A concordance for transformative clinical insights.

**Good:** The Research Kit pairs Oralable with ANR for Dual A. Kits stay gated until charge-to-temple clears. Target: five units to Pedro by 31 Aug 2026.
